          "label": "1. Why You Can't Eat Without Watching Something",
          "detail": "Explains sensory pairing and cue-conditioned dopamine loops: your brain has linked the act of chewing with passive video consumption, making quiet eating feel agonizingly under-stimulating. It reveals how decoupling them resets your satiety signals.\nHook (en): Stop eating until you find a video?\nWhy: Hyper-relatable everyday modern habit with millions of comments on TikTok and Reels admitting they let their food get cold searching for a video. High comment engagement and immediate self-identification drive retention."
        },
        {
          "id": "why-you-feel-guilty-resting",
          "label": "2. Why Doing Nothing Makes You Anxious",
          "detail": "Examines the Default Mode Network and 'idleness aversion': when you finally sit down to relax, your brain switches to self-evaluative background processing, interpreting stillness as falling behind. Explains how purposeful active rest silences the false alarm.\nHook (en): Why does resting make you anxious?\nWhy: Massive cultural pain point among younger demographics suffering from chronic burnout and toxic productivity. Triggers intense emotional resonance and high shares to friends who struggle to unwind."
        },
        {
          "id": "why-awkward-silence-feels-longer",
          "label": "3. Why 4 Seconds of Silence Feels Agonizing",
          "detail": "Unpacks the 4-second conversational gap threshold: research shows that a pause longer than four seconds in casual dialogue triggers ancient social-exclusion fear circuitry in the amygdala, making silence feel three times longer than it actually is.\nHook (en): Silence feels awkward in four seconds.\nWhy: Specific numbers in psychological hooks trigger intense curiosity. The video can physically demonstrate a calibrated 4-second pause, visually and auditorily gripping the viewer and proving the effect in real time."
        }
